{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And I will establish My covenant with you. And you shall eat old store produce.</b> A covenant was needed for the produce of the sabbatical year and the Jubilee, that it not rot — in the manner that Rashi explained regarding the covenant of Noah (Genesis 6:18). Therefore it says 'and you shall eat old store produce' — from two years." "And by way of allusion he said: even though it will happen at one of the times that I will cut a new covenant with you, nevertheless the old covenant too will not be annulled; rather it will be subordinate and the new covenant primary, and 'you shall bring out the old because of the new' — as it is written (Jeremiah 23:7–8): '״וְלֹא יֹאמְרוּ עוֹד חַי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלָה מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם כִּי אִם חַי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלָה וַאֲשֶׁר הֵבִיא מִכָּל הָאֲרָצוֹת״.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 12a): not that the Exodus from Egypt will be uprooted entirely, but it will be subordinate and the ingathering of the exiles primary, for the wonders of Egypt will be subordinate to the great wonders that HaShem will perform in the future, when He cuts a new covenant with Israel. And by this the meaning of the verse is settled, for its words appeared to contradict one another: since it says 'and you shall eat old store produce,' and afterward it says 'and you shall bring out the old because of the new' — for 'old' implies that you bring it out before it becomes 'old store produce.'" "And because he alluded to the redemption and to the exchange, he returned and alluded to the fact that Israel will not be consumed in the length of the exile, in His saying 'and I will set My dwelling in your midst and My soul will not abhor you' — for 'dwelling' means the Tabernacle of Testimony, which is called מִשְׁכָּן because it is pledged (נִתְמַשְׁכֵן) against the debt of Israel. And it is the way of the world that anyone who gives a pledge for his debt — the creditor does not collect from the persons. Thus HaShem gave Israel the מִשְׁכָּן in order to pledge it if they sin, and to exhaust His wrath against wood and stones; and through the fact that He has a place to collect His debt from real property — 'My soul will not abhor you,' that is to say, I will not need to fix My collection against persons (cf. Proverbs 22:23). And what is said in the curses, 'and My soul will abhor you,' means that if even after the destruction they do not accept reproof — for it says first 'and I will break the pride of your might,' which Rashi explained as the Temple, and afterward it is written 'and if despite this you will not listen to Me,' etc. — concerning this He said: since the pride of your might has already been destroyed and again I have no place to collect My debt except from the persons, therefore it says 'and My soul will abhor you.' And what is written 'I have not rejected them nor abhorred them to destroy them' (26:44), means specifically an abhorrence of destruction He will not inflict upon them; but an unspecified abhorrence of persons there will be, if even after the destruction they do not take reproof."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {2 "The parenthetical reference ע״פ משלי כב כג is rendered as 'cf. Proverbs 22:23' since it is cited ad sensum rather than verbatim."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:49.932199555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}